\paragraph{Scoring window}
The deterministic regression gates, Table~\ref{tab:sim_results_rms_dir_basic},
and the ensemble validation protocol all score the same trailing window
$T_{\mathrm{RMS}}=900$~s, the final \SI{900}{s} of each \SI{1200}{s}
run, thereby excluding startup while retaining a long
steady-state record.  The runner reports per-axis and 3D displacement RMS,
roll/pitch/yaw RMS, bias errors, wave-direction accuracy against the generator
azimuth, and diagnostic tuning variables $(f,\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S)$.  For the
non-stationary case it additionally scores three disjoint intervals inside the
same window, for the reason given below.

\paragraph{Paired Monte Carlo and adaptation ablations}
Ten predeclared repetitions independently vary wave realization, IMU noise and random
walk, and residual-error initialization.  Each seed triplet is shared by OU--II
and OU--III and by all ablations.  Kinematically projected surrogates apply a
common random Fourier phase to world velocity and attitude over the
\SIrange{0.02}{1.60}{Hz} fundamental-plus-bound-harmonic band.  Displacement
and acceleration are analytically derived from the same velocity spectrum;
body-frame specific force, angular rate, and quaternion are then reconstructed.
This preserves the retained primitive auto- and cross-spectra without
redistributing finite-record boundary leakage between incompatible derivative
channels.

\paragraph{Inference, and what it does and does not license}
The declared primary endpoint is the seed-level mean normalized
vertical-displacement RMS error over the four stationary JONSWAP seas, compared
between OU--III and OU--II at matched seeds.  It is recorded as
\texttt{primary\_endpoint} in the versioned protocol block of the result
bundle, which is what makes the declaration checkable rather than asserted;
the word \emph{declared} is used in preference to \emph{preregistered}, since
the study carries no external timestamp.  Everything else reported here --
per-scenario contrasts, the adaptation and channel ablations, the covariance
control, the PM--Stokes ensemble, the transition intervals, and the direction
metrics -- is descriptive.

Scenario summaries give the sample mean with the sample standard deviation
across the ten seed triplets; a quoted $a\pm b$ is therefore a spread, not an
interval estimate for the mean.  Intervals are
\OUValidationBootstrapMethod{} with $B=\OUValidationBootstrapResamples$
resamples drawn from a \OUValidationBootstrapRNG{} generator seeded at
$\OUValidationStatsSeed$, so every interval in this paper is reproducible from
the committed rows.  Paired comparisons resample the seed-level differences
rather than the two arms independently, which is what preserves the pairing.
No correction for multiplicity is applied across the
\OUValidationPairedComparisons{} paired comparisons this study computes: each
interval is a per-comparison statement, and the confirmatory claim rests on the
single primary endpoint.  Descriptive intervals should be read accordingly, and an
isolated interval that just excludes zero is weak evidence on its own.

Standardized paired effect sizes (Cohen's $d_z$, Hedges' $g_z$) are reported
for completeness but are deliberately secondary.  With strong pairing and
$n=10$ the denominator is a small-sample standard deviation of differences, so
$d_z$ is both large and imprecisely estimated; the raw paired difference in
percentage points and its bootstrap interval are the primary statement of
effect magnitude throughout.

\paragraph{Four constructions on the primary endpoint}
\label{par:companion-inference}%
A percentile bootstrap at $n=10$ resamples ten numbers, and its interval is
narrow because the pairing is strong rather than because the ensemble is large.
The confirmatory endpoint is therefore reported under four constructions on the
same ten paired differences: the percentile bootstrap of the paired mean, a
Student-$t$ interval on those differences, an exact sign test, and an exact
paired randomization (sign-flip) test enumerated over all
$\OUValidationNormalizedRandomizationPatterns$ sign patterns.  The Student-$t$
interval is $[\OUValidationNormalizedTLow,\OUValidationNormalizedTHigh]$
percentage points ($t=\OUValidationNormalizedTStatistic$,
$p=\OUValidationNormalizedTP$), slightly wider than the bootstrap interval, as
a small-sample $t$ interval should be.
$\OUValidationNormalizedSignNegative$ of the
$\OUValidationStationaryPairs$ seed-level differences favour OU--III, giving
$p=\OUValidationNormalizedSignP$ by the sign test and
$p=\OUValidationNormalizedRandomizationP$ by the randomization test.  Both
exact p-values sit at the floor $2^{-(n-1)}$ that $n=10$ imposes: with ten
pairs no exact paired test can report anything smaller, whichever direction the
data take, and that floor is a property of the design rather than a measure of
the evidence.  These are four readings of one ensemble, not four
experiments, and they agree only about repeatability inside this simulator.

What none of them contains is uncertainty from the simulation model itself,
sensor-model misspecification, the choice of spectral family, the coefficient
values, vessel geometry, update cadence, real hardware vibration, or
environmental disturbance.  Those enter the result as fixed choices, not as
sampled variation, so an interval this narrow should be read as \emph{the
algorithmic difference is stable across realizations of this simulator}, and
not as a bound on the difference a field deployment would see.  Thirty to fifty
seed triplets would estimate the seed-level variance itself more stably; ten
establishes the sign and the approximate magnitude, which is what the primary
claim rests on.  The statistics are recomputed from the archived rows by
\texttt{tools/ou\_validation.py -{}-restat-from}, so a reader can reproduce every
interval in this section, or substitute a different construction, without
re-running a single simulator replay.

\paragraph{Adaptation-channel ablation}
\label{par:channel-ablation}%
Comparing Adaptive with FixedNominal moves three parameters at once, and the
deployed law $r_S=\clip(0.35\,\sigma_{aw}\tau^{3},0.4,400)$ ties the third to the
first two.  That comparison therefore cannot say whether the vertical benefit
comes from adapting the OU process itself or from adapting the integral
regularization.  Two additional OU--III modes complete the $2\times2$
factorial.  \emph{$r_S$ only} freezes $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ at the
FixedNominal point while $r_S$ continues to adapt; \emph{OU only} freezes $r_S$
there while $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ continue to adapt.  In both, the tuner
keeps running and keeps deriving $r_S$ from its \emph{live} $\tau$ and
$\sigma_{aw}$ estimates, so freezing the OU channel on the way to the filter
does not implicitly freeze $r_S$ through the coupled law -- which is precisely
the confound the ablation exists to remove.  All four modes share the deployed
covariance policy.  OU--II regularizes its second-order chain with
$(r_{p0},r_{v0})$ rather than a single integral scale, so the channel ablation
is defined for OU--III only.

\paragraph{Second spectral family and direction scoring}
The four PM--Stokes seas of Table~\ref{tab:scenarios} are scored over the same
window, seed triplets, and primary modes as the JONSWAP ensemble.  PM--Stokes
carries third-order bound harmonics that JONSWAP does not, so it is reported as
a separate declared ensemble and is \emph{not} pooled into the primary
aggregate; pooling would silently redefine the confirmatory comparison.
Direction is scored against the generator azimuth carried by each record.
Because the propagation axis is defined modulo \SI{180}{\degree}, the reported
axial error is the magnitude of the difference between the circular-mean
estimate and that azimuth, alongside the sample-wise axial RMS error and the
axial dispersion.  Travel sense is reported as the share of samples in
whichever sense class the estimator commits to, and the share below the
confidence and amplitude thresholds.  It is deliberately not reported as a
correctness rate: the sense classes are defined against the axis representative
the estimator returns, which carries no fixed relation to the generator
azimuth, so these records cannot say which committed sense is the physical one
(Sec.~\ref{sec:observer-comparison}).

\paragraph{Definition of the fixed operating points}
\label{par:fixed-points}
The adaptation study compares the online \emph{Adaptive} filter against two
frozen-parameter references.  Both are produced by the same procedure: the
adaptive filter is run once on a noise-free, unrandomized \SI{1200}{s} record;
its final $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ are read out; the regularization scale is
computed algebraically from
$r_S=\clip(0.35\,\sigma_{aw}\tau^{3},\,0.4,\,400)$ for OU--III and from
$r_{p0}=\clip(0.6\,\sigma_{aw}\tau^{2},\,0.05,\,150)$ together with
$r_{v0}=\clip(1.1\,\sigma_{aw}\tau,\,0.01,\,40)$ for OU--II; and the resulting
triple is frozen once the filter
reaches its normal Live stage.  The modes differ only in \emph{which} record
supplies that triple.  \emph{FixedNominal} uses the $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$,
$T_p=\SI{5.7}{s}$ record for every scenario.  \emph{FixedOracle} uses the
record of the sea actually being scored, and the known final-sea record for
the transition.

FixedOracle is therefore a \emph{scenario-calibrated fixed reference}, not an
optimum: it is selected from prior knowledge of the sea state, never by
searching against reference displacement error, so it is a lower bound on
mismatch rather than on achievable error.  It is not deployable, because it
requires both the sea state and a noise-free calibration record in advance.
Table~\ref{tab:ou_fixed_points} lists every frozen triple used in the study.
Internally the filter derives the applied anisotropic values from the listed
vertical/base parameters: OU--III uses
$(1.87\sigma_{aw},1.87\sigma_{aw},\sigma_{aw})$ for the stationary
acceleration standard deviation and
$\mat{R}_S=\diag(r_S,r_S,r_S)^2$; OU--II uses $1.5\sigma_{aw}$
horizontally and an isotropic $r_{p0}$.

\paragraph{Covariance policy and its control}
The filter re-aligns the latent-acceleration marginal $\mat{P}_{a_w a_w}$ with
its stationary covariance $\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}}$ once per
adaptation period, preserving the cross-covariances already learned.  This is
a deliberate bounded covariance inflation: it prevents the marginal from
settling far below the level the process model treats as stationary, which
keeps the accelerometer gain responsive when the sea state changes.  It is not
free, because it discards posterior information at the adaptation cadence.

That policy interacts with the ablation.  Applied only inside the adaptation
path, it would run in Adaptive and never in a fixed mode that stops re-tuning,
so an Adaptive-versus-Fixed comparison would confound \emph{whether the
parameters adapt} with \emph{whether part of the covariance is periodically
re-aligned}.  The re-alignment is therefore driven independently of the tuner,
so all three primary modes apply it identically and differ only in whether the
operating point adapts.  To price the policy itself, the study additionally
runs \emph{AdaptiveHeldCovariance} and \emph{FixedNominalHeldCovariance},
which repeat their partner modes with the periodic re-alignment switched off
so that $\mat{P}_{a_w a_w}$ is aligned only at discrete reconfiguration events
-- construction, entry to Live, and an explicit fixed-tuning command.  Each
pair differs in exactly one factor
(Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_covsync}).

\paragraph{Construction and limits of the non-stationary case}
The non-stationary realization is a $C^2$ quintic crossfade between two
\emph{independently} phase-randomized stationary records: the
$(H_s,T_p)=(\SI{1.5}{m},\SI{5.7}{s})$ record and the long-period
$H_s=\SI{8.5}{m}$ record scaled to an $H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$,
$T_p=\SI{11.4}{s}$ endpoint.  With
$x=\clip\!\big((t-t_0)/(t_1-t_0),0,1\big)$ and
$w(x)=10x^{3}-15x^{4}+6x^{5}$, displacement is blended directly while velocity
and acceleration carry the exact derivative cross terms,
\begin{align}
  \vct{p} &= (1-w)\vct{p}_0 + w\vct{p}_1, \\
  \vct{v} &= (1-w)\vct{v}_0 + w\vct{v}_1 + \dot{w}\,(\vct{p}_1-\vct{p}_0), \\
  \vct{a} &= (1-w)\vct{a}_0 + w\vct{a}_1
             + 2\dot{w}\,(\vct{v}_1-\vct{v}_0)
             + \ddot{w}\,(\vct{p}_1-\vct{p}_0),
\end{align}
so the record remains kinematically closed throughout the blend.  The
controlled case uses $t_0,t_1=420,780$~s; the rapid degradation case of
Sec.~\ref{sec:ou-robustness} uses $585,615$~s about the same midpoint.

This is a crossfade, not a physically evolving JONSWAP spectrum, and four
consequences must be stated rather than assumed away.  First, both spectra
coexist during the blend, so the sea can be bimodal and the frequency tracker
may switch between components; there is no single intermediate $T_p$.  Second,
because the two records are independent their variances add, so the midpoint
height is
$H_s\approx\sqrt{0.5^{2}(1.5)^{2}+0.5^{2}(4.0)^{2}}\approx\SI{2.14}{m}$,
not the \SI{2.75}{m} a linear $H_s$ ramp would give.  Third, the
\SI{900}{s} score covers $300$--$420$~s of the pure start sea, $420$--$780$~s
of blend, and $780$--$1200$~s of the pure endpoint sea, so \pct{47} of the
scored record is the endpoint sea alone.  The endpoint-calibrated FixedOracle
is favored by that composition, and the controlled and rapid cases contain
different amounts of pure-start, mixture, and pure-end data.

Fourth, and consequently, a single number normalized by the final
$H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$ is not comparable with a stationary $Z/H_s$ score: for
\pct{13} of the window the sea is \SI{1.5}{m} and for the blend it is a
lower-energy mixture, so the same absolute error is divided by a height the
record does not yet have.  The transition is therefore also scored on the three
disjoint intervals above and reported in absolute metres, together with the
scale-free ratio $Z/Z_{\mathrm{ref}}$ in which $Z_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is the RMS of
the reference vertical displacement over the same interval
(Table~\ref{tab:ou_transition_segments}).  The whole-window $Z/H_s$ figure is
retained only for continuity with earlier reporting.  Taken together, the
transition comparison measures behavior over a specified non-stationary
excursion; it does not isolate adaptation rate, and
Sec.~\ref{sec:observer-comparison} reports a single-realization time series
rather than resting on the aggregate alone.

Machine-readable raw data, statistics, seed manifests, LaTeX tables, and plots
are generated by \texttt{tools/ou\_validation.py}.  The completed
full-protocol results are reported in Sec.~\ref{sec:observer-comparison}; the
versioned raw rows, summaries, paired effects, and manifest are retained with
the source tree.

